sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch03-10> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Very different organisms are linked by chains of interaction, so that the abundance of one species can determine the frequency of another through intermediate species; for example, cats may control the frequency of red clover and heartsease by way of field-mice and humble-bees.";
    dc:type "mechanism";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Chains of interaction link distant species' abundances";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter III, “Struggle for Existence”; section “Complex relations of all animals and plants throughout nature”. Scope: terrestrial communities of plants, pollinators, and their predators"
}
